    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/5/75332.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 78,
    "turn_text": "Well, it's sort of emblematic of the whole week. I mean, if you allow me just sort of back up a little bit, you know, the American people are frustrated with the direction of the country. You know, we all know Washington's broken. I believe that's why Republicans were putting control in the Congress in the first place. And we promise that we do things differently and the voters gave us a very slim majority. And all Republicans agree that we need to do things differently than they've been done before. Some Republicans believe that we needed certain assurances, we needed rules changes, we need structure in place to guarantee that Speaker McCarthy would operate differently than a Speaker Pelosi or even former Republican speakers. Under Speaker Pelosi, the House was a dictatorship. Most bills were written in her office. We were given an up or down vote, but we really didn't have a voice. We really weren't allowed to offer amendments and have real debate. And so there was a number of Republicans who just didn't trust Speaker McCarthy to be different, even though he promised he would. And so we started this week in a real bad place. There's a lot of distrust. A lot of people are assigning different motivations to members. But over the course of the week, we had hundreds of discussions. We had different small groups. We've had one-on-one discussions. And what happened, the result was we built a lot of trust. And I think by hashing out some of the tough things we got to deal with this year, like the debt ceiling, by hashing those things out in advance about how we'll deal with them, I think we're in a really good place. But again, I think because everyone thought we were done and then some people felt like Matt Gates in particular didn't do what they thought he was going to do, I think that was just a moment where those tensions built up all week kind of bubbled up and But I think in retrospect now, I've saw interviews that Mike Rogers and Matt Gaetz both gave this weekend where they both said, look, I respect the other person. Apology was made and apology was accepted. I think we're moving forward much stronger. uh... as bad as things were i think we've got to the other side and i think the country will benefit from it",
